> The code primarily focuses on extracting and estimating key adaptation-related metrics from a dataset of literature review entries. These metrics include Adaptation Effectiveness Ratios, Benefit-Cost Ratios (BCRs), and Economic Internal Rates of Return (EIRRs).
> 
> **Methodological Overview:**
> 
> 1. **Data Preparation and Processing:**
>     - Load and prepare the dataset using helper functions, `get.adapt.df`, `get.row.values`, and others from the loaded R scripts.
>     - Fill missing values by interpolating missing value ranges (using calculated averages from low and high estimates).
> 
> 2. **Transformation and Standardization:**
>     - Adaptation expressions from literature are standardized into a set of defined expressions, e.g., "AvoidedImpact / BaselineImpact" for Adaptation Ratios.
>     - Categories and expressions are standardized and problematic data points or units inconsistencies are filtered out or corrected.
> 
> 3. **Metric Extraction and Estimation:**
>     - Extract key metrics directly when available in standard expressions.
>     - When direct values for metrics like BCRs and Adaptation Ratios are not available, they are inferred using optimization (e.g., `optim()`) to fit data points to relationships defined by expressions. This accounts for cases where multiple components like costs and benefits are involved, estimating using a blend of known values and inferred relationships.
>     - Special mathematical consideration is given to adapting assumed discount rates and expressing monetary values appropriately.
> 
> 4. **Expression Analysis:**
>     - The extracted and inferred metrics are organized into a wider format, suitable for analysis. Multiple expressions can be associated with each source-entry, requiring checks on consistency.
>     - The statistical distributions of key metrics across predefined sectors are examined, and data are plotted to understand the spread and central tendencies of these ratios and rates.
> 
> 5. **Validation and Reporting:**
>     - Verify the consistency and plausibility of extracted metrics by checking common combinations and calibrating against the expected values.
>     - Invise checks are performed using linear models to explore potential biases or patterns, like sample types or expression type influences.
> 
> Overall, the code robustly aggregates and transforms disparate literature data into cohesive and standardized metrics for adaptation effectiveness. It extensively cleans, normalizes, and augments this data by combining directly reported values with inferred calculations, enabling meaningful comparisons and insights into adaptation strategies' economic viability.
